# rts2-viewer exposure modes for OVIS, the long-slit imaging spectrograph,
# camera C1; the guider, C2, has ovis-guider.modes (see viewer-scripting.md
# next to this file for how to write modes). Enable with
#   [viewer]
#   modes = /usr/share/rts2/viewer-modes/ovis.modes
# in rts2.ini, or rts2-viewer --modes <file>.
#
# Taken from python/sites/ovis/ovis-acquire.sh: on the camera, FILTA is
# N (nothing) or grism and FILTB open or jogurt (the slit); W1 is the
# spectrograph device with the NEON calibration lamp, which switches at
# once. Every mode sets all three itself, so it does not depend on what
# ran before. Bare value names (FILTA=...) go to the camera the mode runs
# on.
#
# Every C1 frame except Simple image and Slit image is preceded by a flood: neon through the open beam
# (FILTA=N, FILTB=open) for Calib time, saturating the chip so the next
# frame shows no residual (RBI) of what was on it before. C1 (G2-1600,
# KAF-1603ME) has no preflash to do this in the camera. The script
# language cannot delete a frame, so the floods are saved like the rest:
# they are the frames with FILTA=N, FILTB=open and NEON on in the header,
# and can be deleted afterwards. [MJ 2026-10-26]
#
# Spectral sequence is ovis-acquire.sh in one mode, with a flood at its
# start as well (ovis-acquire.sh began with the identification images):
# Exposure is the full spectrum, Calib time (usually 20 s) the second
# identification image, the short spectrum, the arc and the floods.
# Spectrum and Lambda calib are its two parts, each with its flood, and do
# exactly what the sequence does there: Spectrum is flood, short spectrum
# (Calib time), full spectrum (Exposure); Lambda calib is flood, arc.
# Image + flood and Dark put a flood before their frame too. Flood alone
# is there for anything done by hand.
#
# Added at OVIS, 2026-10-26: Simple image (a plain frame, no flood, no
# filter set) and Slit image (the slit in, no flood) for quick looks, and
# Lambda calib's arc for Exposure time, not Calib time, so the arc can be
# longer than the flood. [MJ 2026-10-26]
#
# The arc after the second flood is taken through the slit and grism.
# ovis-acquire.sh took it with the beam still open from the flood (FILTA=N,
# FILTB=open), i.e. a second flood, not an arc spectrum. [MJ 2026-10-26]
#
# No NCLEARS, though ovis-acquire.sh sets 20 after each flood: the gxccd
# driver passes it to the camera only with a PREFLASH, and C1 (G2-1600,
# KAF-1603ME) has no preflash hardware (GBP_PREFLASH 0, asked 2026-10-26),
# so it never did anything. [MJ 2026-10-26]
#
# Binning is not set (ovis-acquire.sh sets 2x2): use the viewer's panel.
# No flat lamp at OVIS.

[Simple image]
color = grey
script = E $EXPTIME

[Slit image]
color = blue
script = FILTB=jogurt E $EXPTIME

[Image + flood]
color = cyan
script = W1.NEON=on FILTA=N FILTB=open E $CALTIME
  W1.NEON=off E $EXPTIME

[Spectral sequence]
color = green
script = W1.NEON=on FILTA=N FILTB=open E $CALTIME
  W1.NEON=off E 1 E $CALTIME
  FILTB=jogurt E 1
  W1.NEON=on FILTA=N FILTB=open E $CALTIME
  W1.NEON=off FILTB=jogurt FILTA=grism E $CALTIME
  E $EXPTIME
  FILTA=N FILTB=open W1.NEON=on E $CALTIME
  FILTB=jogurt FILTA=grism E $CALTIME
  W1.NEON=off FILTA=N FILTB=open

[Spectrum]
color = green
script = W1.NEON=on FILTA=N FILTB=open E $CALTIME
  W1.NEON=off FILTB=jogurt FILTA=grism E $CALTIME
  E $EXPTIME

[Lambda calib]
color = purple
script = W1.NEON=on FILTA=N FILTB=open E $CALTIME
  FILTB=jogurt FILTA=grism E $EXPTIME
  W1.NEON=off

[Flood]
color = yellow
script = W1.NEON=on FILTA=N FILTB=open E $CALTIME W1.NEON=off

[Dark]
color = red
script = W1.NEON=on FILTA=N FILTB=open E $CALTIME
  W1.NEON=off D $EXPTIME
